Z||000003||superXmotorsports||10-13-1999||08:58 PM||||The widest I have run on the front is a 225/55-16 on a 16X7.5 wheel.  On a stock rim I doubt a 245 would work.  But I could be wrong...||||reg||4
Z||000004||Kage86GT4SP||10-13-1999||09:12 PM||||A few days ago, I put 15X7's (off a plymouth Laser)with 235.60.15's on my SE... not a sound... so I assume no rubbing.<P>K.||||reg||1
Z||000005||87FieroGT||10-13-1999||10:41 PM||||I've got a better one for you. Just yesterday I got my new rims and tires installed at Discount Tire Company. They are 16" Konig Rush rims with 245/45/ZR 16's on them. The guy at the store thought htey may not fit, but we trial fitted the tires and they fit perfectly. No rubbing and about an inch of space to spare. I plan to try and take some pictures this weekend so that I can get them up on the web. I must say though that My 87' GT has never looked any sweeter.||||reg||1
Z||000006||superXmotorsports||10-13-1999||10:57 PM||||I have a set of 17" Konig Rush on my 99 Eclipse Spyder.  The bolt pattern is 5X100 also!  What offset was used?  I'd love to see those pics when you get them.||||reg||4
Z||000007||superXmotorsports||10-13-1999||10:59 PM||||I have a set of 17" Konig Rush on my 99 Eclipse Spyder.  The bolt pattern is 5X100 also!  What offset was used?  I'd love to see those pics when you get them.||||reg||4
Z||000008||88 Fiero||10-13-1999||11:06 PM||||GT...in my experience wheels wise on a Fiero....You can shove a 255/35/17 in the rear of a Fiero but you need to go Coils Over and the front is 235/40/17 with stock shocks. If you do not want to go Coils Over the widest you can go using the stock struts is 245's...that's as far as you can go. I have friends with this set-ups. My wheels are coming in soon from Italy and I can't wait. The rears are 17X9 and I am thinking of shoving 265/35/17 instead of 255 will see...and the fronts are 17X7 1/2 in which I will use 235/40/17. Although an 18 wheel will fit on a Fiero but the down side is that your going to ruin your bearings...||||reg||1
Z||000009||Shiner||10-13-1999||11:28 PM||||Just make sure the tires really are close to the specified width.  Tire manufacturers (especially the generic, cheaper ones) lie about that all the time.  My Firestone Firehawk SH30 off of my '87 GT is actually WIDER than the "235" off my mothers truck, and mine is only 205mm!  ||||reg||4
Z||000010||SloPonti||10-14-1999||12:23 AM||||i have am 84 coupe with 14x7 custom wheels-i need new tires and i currently have 215/60/14 so can i go with 235 or 245/60/14 and have no problems-i would like to keep the same width all the way around! thanks||||reg||5
Z||000011||GT Bastard||10-14-1999||05:02 AM||||Just curious... how do the struts affect it?  I just swapped in a set of Monroe Roadhandlers... I'm thinking the 245's will work, eh?||||reg||1
Z||000012||88 Fiero||10-14-1999||10:14 AM||||Slo...if you are going with 235 or 245 be sure you don't go with 60 series...the tire will bubble up with the kind of width you have on your wheels. Use 50 or 45 series instead, plus it doesn't look good...<P>GT...the struts have a knuckle that holds the rear spring..You want to be sure the wheels doesn't hit this knuckle. That's why a 15,16 or 17 X 7 1/2 is okay even if you go with a 245's tire. Your wheel will still clear it. The offsets on Fiero's all around 4's is 35MM. The '88 Fieros the rear's are 35MM but the fronts are not. I think they are more 45MM offset. When I used to have my 17 OZ's I have to shave at least 5 to 7 MM off... to make it fit.||||reg||1
Z||000013||fastonpumpgas||10-16-1999||07:43 AM||||I am guess all of these specs are with factory offsets.  I am going to 17's soon.  the best wat to do it is get a straight edge and a tape measure and have at it. I would like to know if any one knows how much rear offset you can put on a 17 inch rim in the front, the tire will be 45 series so I am not worried abou the front offset, just as long as it does go out past the well to far.. 245 meats in the front and 265 in the rear is my goal, even if I have to get costum made wheels   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000014||88 Fiero||10-16-1999||12:42 PM||||fastongas...I don't think you want to go on 245's in the front...reason is that your going to track more and your steering wheel will not be stable...since it's going to track...your steering wheel will be shifting left to right and will vibrate a lot....The specs that I got for the Fiero was base on the Porsche 911's. Even Herb Adams himself when he recommended size for the 16 inch is 225's in the front and 245's in the rear.. Like I said the most you can get away on the front is 235's...||||reg||1
Z||000015||Boxdin||10-16-1999||01:47 PM||||My question is; While my 88 Formula is on jackstands with the wheels off, I can see the rear hub is farther towards the center of the car by an inch at least, that the front hub. If I use the same size new wheel all the way around, wont the fronts stick out farther than the rear?||||reg||1
Z||000016||lowCG||10-16-1999||01:59 PM||||I think '88 has a good point,and unless you've been there,won't make much sense.The rear/mid engined performance cars have never been produced with much of a large tire in the front,just not needed,will be a  headache to drive also,will lose a lot of the feedback when going into a turn,take the bumps harder,bumpsteer.A taller,more narrow tire is usually going to have a more(wider)desirable slip angle.That's basically the amount of angle,from straight ahead,that the tires will effectively achieve grip. ||||reg||1
Z||000017||88 Fiero||10-16-1999||06:40 PM||||Boxdin...my car is also an '88 Formula...the rear offset of the car is 35MM which is very common in after market wheels One Piece Wheels.... On the other hand the front is around 45MM.. Although you can get away with a 42MM...by shaving the 35MM off to 42MM and it will still look very good on the '88 Fiero. But if you try to stick a 35MM wheel in the front, I'll guarantee you it will stick out. I don't think that there's a lot of One Piece wheels that has a 45MM offset.  Majority of them are 35MM. In order to get a correct offset for the '88's you have to go with a 2 or 3 piece wheel in which it would be expensive. I'll will be doing the same thing again with my new wheels coming in next week. Shave the front 17X7 1/2 35MM to a mere 42MM... The rears are 17X9 in which I won't worry about shaving it off although I might need to go Coils Over because it might hit the knuckle of my Struts...||||reg||1
Z||000018||superXmotorsports||10-18-1999||10:13 PM||||I thought I would revive this thread and ask if anyone knows what size and offset I can run in 17" on my 87 GT in the rear?  I saw someone said 255 or 265 tires too.  Thanks.||||reg||5
